{
  "moduleId": "m00205",
  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00205:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Reactions of Alcohols"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "6a6632eb-357d-411a-806b-65f7dab2db37"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00205:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00205:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "Conversion of Alcohols into Alkyl Halides"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00002",
          "segmentId": "m00205:para:para-00002"
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00003",
          "segmentId": "m00205:para:para-00003"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00001",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00205:alt:fig-00001-alt",
              "text": "Tertiary alcohol is protonated by hydrogen chloride or hydrogen bromide, water leaves forming carbocation (S N 1 mechanism), which reacts with halide to form an alkyl chloride or bromide."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_06_001.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00004",
          "segmentId": "m00205:para:para-00004"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00002",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00205:alt:fig-00002-alt",
              "text": "Primary or secondary alcohol reacts with thionyl chloride to form chlorosulfite which forms alkyl chloride. The alcohol reacts with phosphorus tribromide to form dibromo phosphite. This leads to alkyl bromide."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_06_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00002",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00205:title:sect-00002-title",
        "text": "Conversion of Alcohols into Tosylates"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00005",
          "segmentId": "m00205:para:para-00005"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00003",
          "class": "unnumbered",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00205:alt:fig-00003-alt",
              "text": "An alcohol reacts with para-toluenesulfonylchloride in the presence of pyridine to form a tosylate (R O T o S) and pyridine dot hydrogen chloride."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_06_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00006",
          "segmentId": "m00205:para:para-00006"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00004",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00205:caption:fig-00004-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00205:alt:fig-00004-alt",
              "text": "(R)-2-octanol reacts with phosphorus tribromide to form (S)-2-bromooctane. This reacts with sodium ethoxide forming ethyl (R)-1-methylheptyl ether. (R)-2-octanol reacts with para-tosylchloride to form (R)-1-methylheptyl tosylate that forms ethyl (S)-1-methylheptyl ether."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_06_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00003",
      "class": "section-exercises",
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00205:title:sect-00003-title",
        "text": "Problems"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "exercise",
          "id": "exer-00001",
          "problem": {
            "content": []
          },
          "solution": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00004",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00205:title:sect-00004-title",
        "text": "Dehydration of Alcohols to Yield Alkenes"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00008",
          "segmentId": "m00205:para:para-00008"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00005",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00205:alt:fig-00005-alt",
              "text": "1-methylcyclohexanol reacts with hydronium ion, tetrahydrofuran at 50 degree celsius to form 1-methylcyclohexene with 91 percent yield."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_06_006.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00009",
          "segmentId": "m00205:para:para-00009"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00006",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00205:alt:fig-00006-alt",
              "text": "2-methyl-2-butanol reacts with hydronium ion, tetrahydrofuran at 25 degree Celsius to form 2-methyl-2-butene (trisubstituted) as the major product and 2-methyl-1-butene (disubstituted) as the minor product."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_06_007.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00010",
          "segmentId": "m00205:para:para-00010"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00007",
          "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00205:caption:fig-00007-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00205:alt:fig-00007-alt",
              "text": "Three-step mechanism: 1-methylcyclohexanol is protonated by hydronium, water leaves forming tertiary carbocation, deprotonation of adjacent carbon by water results in formation of double bond, produces 1-methylcyclohexene."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_06_009.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00011",
          "segmentId": "m00205:para:para-00011"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00008",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00205:alt:fig-00008-alt",
              "text": "1-methylcyclohexanol reacts with phosphorus oxychloride and pyridine at 0 degree Celsius to form 1-methylcyclohexene with 96 percent yield."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_06_008.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00012",
          "segmentId": "m00205:para:para-00012"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00009",
          "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00205:caption:fig-00009-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00205:alt:fig-00009-alt",
              "text": "Two-step mechanism: cyclohexanol oxygen attacks P O Cl 3, chlorine leaves. Pyradine deprotonates adjacent carbon, electrons force O P O Cl 2 to leave, forming cyclohexene."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_06_010.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00013",
          "segmentId": "m00205:para:para-00013"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00010",
          "class": "unnumbered",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00205:alt:fig-00010-alt",
              "text": "A base reacts with 5-dehydroquinate to form an anion intermediate. This is followed by the release of water to form 5-dehydroshikimate. This further forms tyrosine."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_17_06_011.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00005",
